cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Certified Inference: A Pre-Registered, Exact-Real, Kernel-Checked Next-Token Certificate for a Pretrained LayerNorm Language Model"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it."
authors:
  - family-names: Naughton
    given-names: Brian
date-released: 2026-07-04
license: MIT
abstract: >
  A pre-registered, independently verifiable statistical certificate for the
  exact-real next-token argmax of a real pretrained LayerNorm language model.
  For the exact-real function defined by the published TinyStories-1M float32
  checkpoint, an outward-rounded interval certificate establishes a unique
  full-vocabulary (50,257-token) next-token argmax at precision P <= 256 on
  every prompt of a pre-registered, with-replacement, seed-committed sample of
  n = 1000 windows drawn from a pinned finite token-id corpus C at context
  length 16 — all 1000 re-derived bit-identically by an independent Torch-free
  checker that asserts full-corpus completeness. By a one-sided Hoeffding bound,
  with confidence at least 97.5%, at least 95.70% of C is certifiable under the
  stated engine and escalation policy; in the same frozen run the pinned float32
  harness agreed with the certified exact-real argmax on all 1000 samples,
  yielding a separate lower bound on the joint event. The Hoeffding
  lower-confidence theorem is kernel-checked in Lean 4 with Mathlib (axioms
  propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound); the finite-corpus sampling bridge and
  the harness conformance leg are audited provenance, not formalised. A
  full-vocabulary GPT-2-small certificate set is reported as prestige
  confirmation carrying no population claim.
keywords:
  - formal verification
  - interval arithmetic
  - language models
  - exact-real arithmetic
  - pre-registration
  - Hoeffding bound
  - Lean
  - AI safety
